<body>
<h1>Unit 8: Elasticity</h1><p class="meta">Physics XI  |  NEB (Grade 11)  |  Phy. 101 &nbsp;•&nbsp; Content area: Mechanics</p><p class="summary">Elasticity turns 'how stiff is this material' into a measurable number with units of pressure, and it separates the regimes in which a material returns to its shape from the regimes in which it does not.</p><h2>What the model asserts</h2><ol><li>Stress is force per unit area; strain is fractional change in length; Hooke's law says stress is proportional to strain only within the linear elastic regime.</li><li>Young's modulus E = stress/strain has the dimensions of pressure and belongs to the material, not to the particular specimen you cut from it.</li><li>The stress-strain curve has recognisable regimes: linear elastic, yield, plastic flow, strain hardening, necking and fracture.</li><li>Ductile materials give warning before they fail; brittle materials such as glass and cast iron do not, which is why toughness and ductility are engineered separately.</li><li>The elastic limit is not the tensile strength: the first marks the end of recoverable deformation, the second the maximum stress the material carries.</li><li>The elastic energy stored per unit volume is (1/2) x stress x strain, and Poisson's ratio couples longitudinal strain to lateral contraction.</li><li>Stiffness and strength are different properties: the modulus says how much strain a given stress produces, the ultimate stress says how much stress the material survives, and a material can be strong without being stiff.</li></ol><h2>Conditions, assumptions and limits</h2><ul><li>Hooke's law needs small strains, an isotropic material, a uniform stress state and a fixed temperature; it fails for rubber (strongly non-Hookean), for plastics beyond the yield point, and for composites loaded off-axis.</li><li>Young's modulus depends on temperature and on rate: rubber's modulus rises with strain rate and falls with temperature, which is why a stretched rubber band stiffens when pulled faster.</li><li>Fatigue means cyclic loading below the yield strength can still fracture a component after many cycles, and a static tensile test cannot reveal it.</li><li>A hole or notch concentrates stress: the local peak stress, not the nominal stress, decides failure, so a small crack can dominate a design.</li><li>Poisson's ratio ties lateral strain to longitudinal strain, so no solid can be perfectly incompressible and perfectly isotropic at once; most engineering metals sit near 0.3.</li></ul><h2>Key numbers</h2><table><tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Value</th></tr><tr><td>Young's modulus of steel</td><td>about 200 GPa</td></tr><tr><td>Young's modulus of bone (along the shaft)</td><td>about 18 GPa</td></tr><tr><td>Ultimate tensile strength of mild steel</td><td>about 400 MPa</td></tr><tr><td>Bulk modulus of water</td><td>about 2.2 GPa</td></tr></table><h2>Open questions and mysteries</h2><div class="mystery"><p><b>Can the strength of a material be predicted from its atomic structure alone?</b></p><p class="status">Frontier</p><p>Dislocation theory explains strength qualitatively; quantitative prediction for real materials remains a research problem.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>How do microscopic defects nucleate and grow into the cracks that end a component's life?</b></p><p class="status">Under active scrutiny</p><p>The mechanisms are known in outline, and fatigue-life prediction for specific parts remains an engineering challenge.</p></div><h2>Inquiry prompts</h2><ol><li>Plot a stress-strain curve for a steel wire or a plastic strip and label every regime you can identify.</li><li>Design an experiment to find Young's modulus of a bamboo strip, and state the dominant uncertainty in your result.</li><li>Explain, with an example, why a sharp crack is more dangerous than a round hole of the same size.</li></ol><h2>Further reading</h2><ul><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/12-3-stress-strain-and-elastic-modulus">OpenStax: Stress, Strain and Elastic Modulus</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/preface">OpenStax: University Physics Volume 1</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/12-1-conditions-for-static-equilibrium">OpenStax: Conditions for Static Equilibrium</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/12-4-elasticity-and-plasticity">OpenStax: Elasticity and Plasticity</a></li></ul><p class="note">Physics XI — Academic Companion — a companion brief, not a replacement for the prescribed NEB text. The open questions describe live research frontiers; they are enrichment, not a claim that the established school-level model is invalid. Check the prescribed text and syllabus for examinable scope.</p>
</body>
